-- The application used to store text through html-escape: ampersands, angle
-- brackets, slashes and accented letters were written as HTML entities, and
-- every page unescaped them again.  Text is now stored as the reader typed
-- it and escaped once when a page is written, so what is already stored is
-- decoded here, in one pass, in every column that was written escaped.
--
-- `&amp;' is decoded last: a reader who typed `&amp;' had it stored as
-- `&amp;amp;', and one decode leaves the text they typed.
--
-- The entities are the set kracht's html-escape writes, plus the four
-- (/, *, $, %) an older kracht wrote.  The decode is batched rather than one
-- long expression: the SQLite this migration runs through overflows its
-- parser stack past roughly 28 nested calls.
--
-- Columns deliberately left alone hold plain text already: a ticket's own
-- statuses, a saved filter's query, a time entry's comment and an uploaded
-- file's name were never escaped on the way in.


